{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261118T181719Z-044c12d5", "ts": "2026-11-18T18:24:14Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.8.2-dev.1", "branch": "fix/triage-batch-2026-11-18", "build": "3682719168c6"}, "body": {"base_commit": "3682719168c6305396290182baed4e52dc85df73", "base_tree": "5f4f1f1ab012ff6bf964390a3dfd2e4c838ffd3c", "head_tree": "7a7d5cc97778213e6f4048bfcf2350a8e7be36d0", "head_commit": "90268701af9637c535911f4a1f6fcabc41afcb8f", "dispatch_commit": "90268701af9637c535911f4a1f6fcabc41afcb8f", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["documentation/issues/1041-design.md", "documentation/issues/1041-requirements.md", "documentation/post-sync-advisory-spec.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/advisory_store.py", "plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py", "plugin/lib/testament_evidence_probes.py", "plugin/methodology/delegation.md", "plugin/methodology/reflection.md", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "plugin/skills/methodology/SKILL.md", "tests/test_advisory_evidence_pins.py", "tests/test_advisory_store.py", "tests/test_git_hygiene_apply.py", "tests/test_git_hygiene_merge.py", "tests/test_testament_evidence_probes.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/changes/advisory-evidence-pins.md", ".prawduct/changes/clear-verdict-delegates.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-tidy-submodule-worktrees.md", ".prawduct/changes/testament-evidence-wrapper-scripts.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "documentation/issues/1041-design.md", "documentation/issues/1041-requirements.md", "documentation/post-sync-advisory-spec.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/lib/advisory_store.py", "plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py", "plugin/lib/testament_evidence_probes.py", "plugin/methodology/delegation.md", "plugin/methodology/reflection.md", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", "plugin/skills/methodology/SKILL.md", "tests/test_advisory_evidence_pins.py", "tests/test_advisory_store.py", "tests/test_git_hygiene_apply.py", "tests/test_git_hygiene_merge.py", "tests/test_testament_evidence_probes.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/changes/advisory-evidence-pins.md", ".prawduct/changes/clear-verdict-delegates.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-tidy-submodule-worktrees.md", ".prawduct/changes/testament-evidence-wrapper-scripts.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Missing", "severity": "warning", "title": "The clear-verdict check and its telemetry rule still treat any harness background task as making DO NOT CLEAR right, which #1190 has just made false for a delegate the notes describe", "recommendation": "Why it broke: the #1190 rule changed what justifies a hold, but the check that scores holds still reads the old premise. The new session-hygiene rule says a running delegate is SAFE TO CLEAR once the forward notes say what it is doing and why. `_clear_observations` still sets `work_live` True for any harness `background_tasks` entry, and its docstring still says background work 'makes DO NOT CLEAR true'. `cmd_stop` lets YOUR TURN + DO NOT CLEAR pass unrecorded whenever `work_live` is True, and governance-telemetry.md scores a clear-verdict firing with `work_live` as a slip because 'the hold was right'. So a needless hold beside a documented delegate is never recorded, and `stats` misclassifies the COMPLETE cases. The owner cannot measure whether agents adopted the new SAFE TO CLEAR. The two sentences this diff rewrote also misstate what the hook can see. A running delegate whose purpose is missing from the notes is not invisible: it is a background task, so `work_live` reads True. What the hook cannot see is whether the notes cover a delegate, and a finished delegate whose output nobody has read. Scope: class. Fix every reader that takes `OBSERVED_WORK_LIVE` to mean 'the hold was right': the YOUR TURN pass in `cmd_stop`, the clear-verdict slip rule in `stats`/governance-telemetry.md, and the session-hygiene.md Stop-hook paragraph that describes the pass. Either narrow the verdict to what still justifies a hold (a live review, or background work with `notes_present` not True), or state in each place that `work_live` over-approximates. Then correct the two rewritten parentheticals.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "The submodule proof checks HEAD and branches only, but the forced removal deletes the whole submodule repository: stashes, tags and a non-absorbed submodule repo are lost unchecked", "recommendation": "Why it broke: the proof lists less than `--force` deletes. `_submodule_hold` runs `rev-list --max-count=1 HEAD --branches --not --remotes`, so a submodule's `refs/stash`, a local tag or a commit reachable only from another ref passes the proof. `git worktree remove --force` then deletes the module repository under `<worktree gitdir>/modules/`, and those commits exist nowhere else. Also, `_module_repos` finds only repositories absorbed under `modules/`. A submodule whose `.git` is an embedded directory in its working tree (for example, `git submodule add` over an existing local clone) is not in `repos`. If the same worktree has one absorbed submodule, `has_submodules` is True and the forced removal deletes the embedded repo too. The status check sees it only when its HEAD differs from the gitlink or it is dirty, not when its unpublished commit is the recorded one. Scope: class (the proof must cover what force deletes). Use `rev-list --all --not --remotes` (it covers stash and tags; a fetched remote tag on a commit no remote branch carries keeps the worktree, which is the safe side). Enumerate submodules the way git's own `validate_no_submodules` does, from the populated gitlinks in the worktree's index, and resolve each one's git dir. Refuse `--force` when any populated gitlink's repository is not one the proof checked. Add a test per escape: a stash in the submodule, and a mixed absorbed plus embedded worktree.", "files": ["plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "'A worktree without submodules is never forced' is the departure's stated safety bound, and no test pins it", "recommendation": "Why it broke: only the forced shape is pinned. The one command assertion is `test_a_sibling_with_a_published_submodule_is_removed_forced`, which pins `--force`. No test asserts that a plain sibling's planned `command`, or `reap`'s argv, omits it. Every check before removal (`_reprove`'s status, `reap`'s status) runs before git does, so all tests would stay green if `_remove_worktree_command` always forced. That would silently remove git's own refusal of a worktree that became dirty after the checks, the 'second guard' its docstring keeps on purpose. 1041-requirements.md (\"A worktree without submodules is still never forced\") and the change record both promise this. Scope: instance. Assert `w.command == (\"git\", \"worktree\", \"remove\", w.target)` in an existing plain-sibling plan test. Pin `reap`'s argv for a worktree with no submodules, either by monkeypatching `_git_write` or by testing `_remove_worktree_command(path, False)` beside it.", "files": ["tests/test_git_hygiene_apply.py", "tests/test_git_hygiene_merge.py", "plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The always-injected digest still carries the old delegate clear rule: nothing tells an onboarded session that a running delegate needs its purpose in the forward notes before SAFE TO CLEAR", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the change rewrote the delegate rule in session-hygiene.md and left plugin/methodology/session-digest.md unchanged. The first 2026-11-18 ruling (.prawduct/owner-rulings.md) says this 'Lands in methodology/session-hygiene.md and the digest's closing-the-turn rule'. The digest's closing section reads 'DO NOT CLEAR only if a clear would kill it' and 'Persist findings-only or delegate output to .prawduct/.handoff-notes.md first'. An in-flight delegate has no output yet, and the guide now says a clear kills no delegate. So an agent working from the digest alone, which is every session that never opens the full guide, closes SAFE TO CLEAR beside a running delegate without writing what it is for. Its completion then lands as the 'Task 23 complete' the owner described. The consumer note promises the notes requirement to governed repos, but only the on-demand guide carries it. Learnings rule: 'A new framework-wide DEFAULT must land in `session-digest.md` \u2014 the only surface every onboarded repo re-reads'. Fix: change the digest's persist clause to cover a running delegate, e.g. 'Persist findings-only output, or what a running delegate is doing and why, to ...'. Keep the edit inside the digest's 10,000-character wall (test_plugin_methodology_digest.py).", "files": ["plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "session-hygiene.md defines a killable delegate two ways: Precedence says 'a delegate whose report is the only record of its work', the delegate paragraph says the conversation being the sole record of what it is doing and why", "recommendation": "Instance (Framework Check 8, contradiction within one guide). Why it broke: the Precedence parenthetical was rewritten to a criterion about the delegate's RESULT, while the clear-verdict paragraph keys on its PURPOSE being recorded. That paragraph also says the completion, the report included, still arrives after a clear. An agent applies Precedence first, when it picks YOUR TURN. Read that way, a delegate whose notes already say what it is doing, where its result lands and what it owes still holds DO NOT CLEAR until its report lands. That is the unnecessary hold #1190 removes. Make the parenthetical relational: name 'a delegate the forward notes do not yet describe' and point at the delegate paragraph, so one sentence owns the test.", "files": ["plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md"], "fid": "R-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The clear-verdict check and its telemetry rule still treat any harness background task as making DO NOT CLEAR right, a premise this change falsified for delegates", "recommendation": "Class: every reader that equates 'harness background work' with 'work a clear would kill'. Why it broke: the change made a running delegate clearable once the notes describe it. Code and docs that read a live background task as a justified hold were reworded, not re-derived. Sites: (1) prawduct-hook cmd_stop's clear-verdict branch, which skips recording YOUR TURN \u00b7 DO NOT CLEAR whenever OBSERVED_WORK_LIVE is True. Its comment still says 'beside work a clear would kill'. (2) The _clear_observations docstring, which says harness background work 'makes DO NOT CLEAR true'. Its new parenthetical calls an undescribed delegate 'invisible here', but a running delegate is visible as background work. The case this hook cannot see is a finished delegate whose output was never read. (3) governance-telemetry.md, which still scores clear-verdict as 'a slip when work_live: the hold was right'. Consequence: a YOUR TURN \u00b7 DO NOT CLEAR held beside a running delegate the notes already describe, the exact hold #1190 makes wrong, is never recorded. So stats cannot show whether the fix changed behaviour, and the telemetry doc tells its reader the hold was right. Fix it through one owner: keep recording when work_live comes only from a harness background task (not a live review) and let stats score it with notes_present. Or, if skipping stays deliberate, state that approximation once and make the docstring and telemetry rule say so relationally. Search: OBSERVED_WORK_LIVE, work_in_flight and 'work_live' across plugin/bin, plugin/lib/stats.py and plugin/docs.", "files": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md"], "fid": "R-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "severity": "warning", "title": "The #1190 reversal is recorded in owner-rulings and session-hygiene, but not in the artifact whose requirement it reverses: adhoc-delegation-discovery R10 still says an unreaped delegate is DO NOT CLEAR because it dies with the session", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: the change recorded its why where the deciders read it (owner-rulings.md) and where the rule is stated (session-hygiene.md), but not in the requirements artifact that owns the decision it inverts. `.prawduct/artifacts/adhoc-delegation-discovery.md` R10 (line ~241: 'unreaped is `RUNNING` and `DO NOT CLEAR`; reaped with its debt on disk may be `SAFE TO CLEAR`') and its supporting argument (lines ~76-81 and ~370-375: 'the delegate dies with the session ... R10 stands') state the premise the owner's 2026-11-18 ruling falsified ('background agents do survive clear'). Consequence: anyone who judges session-hygiene.md's new delegate paragraph against its requirement, whether a later builder or a reviewer, reads a departure from R10 with no decision recorded beside it, or restores the old hold from the artifact. #1187 in this same bundle shows the right shape: it recorded its departure beneath R2 in 1041-requirements.md. Do the same under R10: cite the 2026-11-18 ruling, give the new test (a delegate holds the session only while the conversation is the sole record of what it is doing and why), and mark the 'dies with the session' premise superseded where it is argued. Per core learning 'A mechanism change is done when every artifact DESCRIBING it agrees', search for the claim, not the tokens: `git grep -n -i 'dies with the session\\|unreaped' -- .prawduct/artifacts plugin` with archives and change-log history excluded, since they are exempt.", "files": ["plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/adhoc-delegation-discovery.md"], "fid": "R-7"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 7, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "The advisory spec's new exemption ('a type that has never shipped needs no bump') is one the new pin test does not honour", "recommendation": "`test_static_evidence_moves_only_with_its_version` fails on any digest change under an unchanged version, shipped or not. Its remedy says to bump, and the PINS comment forbids re-pinning a digest under the same version. So the spec sentence permits something its enforcer refuses. The sentence also adds an exception to the Versioned rule in the commit that relies on it for the gitignore case. The learnings rule 'Editing a norm's STATEMENT in the same commit as the code it would bless is the amend-to-match-own-code tell' applies if that bullet binds. The 2026-11-18 ruling records only that #1192 is fixed on this branch, not this exemption. Either delete the sentence and keep the exemption in the change record, or record the owner's ruling and make the test's message name the unshipped-type route. The claim itself checks out: `contract-ignored-by-rule` (7727fb34) is on develop only and is in no tag.", "files": ["documentation/post-sync-advisory-spec.md", "tests/test_advisory_evidence_pins.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The wrapper read takes any in-repo file any word names, and counts a line that merely mentions `testament ... run`", "recommendation": "`_scripts` resolves every non-flag word against the repo. So `pytest tests/test_testament_x.py` reads a test file, and a string literal like \"testament run --junit-xml {junit_xml}\" counts as running testament. The same goes for `-c pyproject.toml`, an `echo \"use testament run\"` line, or a CI-only `if` branch. A false 'wired' silences the advisory. It also makes `from_counts_refusal` tell the agent that a bare record runs the declared command on testament, which then runs locally. This fails toward silence on an advisory, so it is not rated a finding. If it is tightened, read only the word the command executes (its first word, or the script argument of `sh`/`bash`/`uv run`).", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_evidence_probes.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "The module docstring still says the refusal 'reads nothing'", "recommendation": "'The recorder hands its resolved declaration to from_counts_refusal, so the refusal reads nothing' is now false. The refusal calls `records_testament(c, root)` and `runs_testament(c, root)`, which read wrapper scripts. Delete the clause or make it relational. This is prose and nothing reads it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_evidence_probes.py"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "`reap` reports the submodule proof's 'could not read' unknowns as KEPT (exit 0), where its other unknowns are FAILED (exit 1)", "recommendation": "`reap` returns FAILED for 'could not read its status' and 'could not list branches', and the CLI exits 1 only on FAILED. The three new unknowns ('could not find its git directory', 'could not read its submodules' status', 'could not read submodule X's commits') come back as KEPT, so an unreadable state exits 0 like a deliberate keep. The outcome is data-safe either way. Consider returning a separate unknown from `_submodule_hold`, so `reap` can map it to FAILED the way its siblings do.", "files": ["plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Unintended", "title": "Governing-Artifact Reconciliation: no governed_by dispositions to reconcile; #1041 R2's departure is recorded but not yet confirmed", "recommendation": "No live plan claims this branch, and no record in the bundle carries a `governed_by:` disposition, so there is nothing to reconcile. Forcing a worktree removal departs from #1041 R2 (\"never escalated to `-D` or `--force`\"). The departure is recorded beneath the clause, which is left intact, and it is reasoned and timely. It is not in the vetoable `[DECISION: \u2026 | \u2026 | user can veto/override]` form and says 'awaiting the owner's confirmation'. The 2026-11-18 ruling approves fixing #1187 without saying how. Land the owner's confirmation (or veto) before merge, somewhere other than the amendment itself. The shipped change record also says 'awaiting'.", "files": ["documentation/issues/1041-requirements.md", "plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "runs_testament's 'wrapper script' is any in-repo file a declared command names, read as shell", "recommendation": "_scripts accepts every non-flag word that resolves to a file inside the repo. `pytest tests/test_x.py` therefore reads a Python test file. _words recurses into quoted strings, so a line like `cmd = \"testament run ...\"` reads as wiring. `make -f Makefile test` reads the Makefile, although the docstring says make targets are not read. Both suppress the advisory, or make from_counts_refusal claim the command is wired. Realistic hits are rare. A tighter predicate would read only the word in program position (after sh/bash/uv run), or a file with a shebang or the executable bit.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_evidence_probes.py"], "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "_remove_worktree_command returns a 'git'-prefixed argv that reap slices with [1:], and the has_submodules decision made at plan time is baked into the Action", "recommendation": "_reprove recomputes _submodule_hold but discards has_submodules. A worktree that gains submodules between the dry run and --apply is removed with the old unforced command, which git refuses (FAILED, the safe direction). The [1:] slice couples reap to the tuple's shape. Returning the arguments without the program name would remove the slice. Recomputing the flag where the command runs would remove the stale bake-in.", "files": ["plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "read-cost: _submodule_hold adds git subprocesses to the offline plan the session-start tidy advisory runs", "recommendation": "It costs one rev-parse per eligible sibling worktree, plus a recursive status and one rev-list per submodule. It runs only for worktrees that already passed every other check, so it is bounded. Unlike the stranded_work.scan inputs the 1041 design calls budgeted, it has no budget. No volume is stated (templates/data-model.md 'Access Paths').", "files": ["plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "The evidence-pin scan recognises only bare-name AdvisoryCandidate(...) calls", "recommendation": "A candidate built as `advisory_store.AdvisoryCandidate(...)`, or through dataclasses.replace, is neither pinned nor counted in DYNAMIC_EVIDENCE_CANDIDATES. No probe does this today, so the scan quietly approves that future shape. Learnings rule: 'A lint that SKIPS what it can't statically read needs a companion asserting all IS readable'. A cheap companion: assert that the count of 'AdvisoryCandidate' tokens in each *_probes.py equals the number of calls the scan classified.", "files": ["tests/test_advisory_evidence_pins.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "Records Pass covered all five oracle files; none clears a bar", "recommendation": "Set covered: .prawduct/changes/advisory-evidence-pins.md, .prawduct/changes/clear-verdict-delegates.md, .prawduct/changes/git-tidy-submodule-worktrees.md, .prawduct/changes/testament-evidence-wrapper-scripts.md, .prawduct/owner-rulings.md. Checked against the source: the gitignore `contract-ignored-by-rule` type is on no release tag (v3.7.2, v3.8.0, v3.8.1) and not on origin/main, only on origin/develop, so 'has not shipped in a release' holds. The pins test does pin only literal or module-constant evidence and counts the dynamic candidates (21). `from_counts_refusal` is the recorder's `--from-counts` refusal (prawduct-hook:5694) and now gets the repo root. The #1041 departure is honestly marked 'awaiting the owner's confirmation'. Owner-rulings entry 1's 'Lands in ... the digest's closing-the-turn rule' is narrowed by entry 2. The digest's closing rule is still consistent with the new delegate test. No edit wanted.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/advisory-evidence-pins.md", ".prawduct/changes/clear-verdict-delegates.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-tidy-submodule-worktrees.md", ".prawduct/changes/testament-evidence-wrapper-scripts.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "'Published' means reachable from a local remote-tracking ref, which the reap does not refresh for the submodule", "recommendation": "`_submodule_hold` runs `rev-list HEAD --branches --not --remotes` against the submodule's own repository. That reads the remote-tracking refs as last fetched, and nothing fetches the submodule first. The change fragment, the 1041 departure and the docstring all say 'on one of its remotes'. That matches what a maintainer would assume except when a tracking ref is stale, for example when a remote branch was deleted after a squash merge. This is a precision note on the prose. Whether stale refs matter for safety is Goal 1's call.", "files": ["plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py"], "oid": "O-11"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "Module docstring still says the refusal 'reads nothing'; it now reads wrapper scripts", "recommendation": "testament_evidence_probes.py's 'Which declaration' paragraph says 'The recorder hands its resolved declaration to :func:`from_counts_refusal`, so the refusal reads nothing.' Since this change the refusal passes `root` to `records_testament`/`runs_testament`, which read any in-repo script a declared command names. The sentence was about not re-reading project-state, which is still true. Make it relational ('reads no declaration of its own') or delete the clause. Nothing reads it, so this is an observation, not a finding.", "files": ["plugin/lib/testament_evidence_probes.py"], "oid": "O-12"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "A sibling worktree kept for its submodules is dropped from the plan silently, like every other keep condition in _sibling_removal", "recommendation": "`--reap` and `_reprove` both report the submodule hold reason. In the dry-run plan and the tidy-available advisory, a merged, idle sibling worktree that holds unpublished submodule commits just does not appear. That matches the function's existing keep conditions, which are also silent. It is worth knowing if a user asks why git-tidy never offers to remove that worktree. No change asked.", "files": ["plugin/lib/git_hygiene.py"], "oid": "O-13"}], "duration_seconds": 660, "scope": null, "scope_chosen_by": "none", "scope_unresolved_cause": "unbound", "scope_reason": "no live plan claims `fix/triage-batch-2026-11-18`", "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": [], "chunks_proposed": [], "base_reviewed": "3682719168c6305396290182baed4e52dc85df73", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/changes/advisory-evidence-pins.md", ".prawduct/changes/clear-verdict-delegates.md", ".prawduct/changes/git-tidy-submodule-worktrees.md", ".prawduct/changes/testament-evidence-wrapper-scripts.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "documentation/issues/1041-design.md", "documentation/issues/1041-requirements.md", "documentation/post-sync-advisory-spec.md", "plugin/docs/governance-telemetry.md", "plugin/docs/project-state-keys.md", "plugin/methodology/delegation.md", "plugin/methodology/reflection.md", "plugin/methodology/session-hygiene.md"